Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://democracy.cambridge.gov.uk"}],"recent":[{"date":"2026-06-23","committee":"Performance, Assets and Strategy Overview and Scrutiny Committee","time":"5.30 pm","status":"Confirmed; Private briefing for members","webcast":false,"url":"https://democracy.cambridge.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-07-07","committee":"Cabinet","time":"5.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://democracy.cambridge.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-07-15","committee":"Performance, Assets and Strategy Overview and Scrutiny Committee","time":"5.30 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://democracy.cambridge.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-07-16","committee":"Council","time":"6.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://democracy.cambridge.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-07-21","committee":"Cabinet","time":"5.30 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://democracy.cambridge.gov.uk"}]},"news":[],"news_drafts":[{"headline":"Homes England strategic partnership bid outcome pending, with \u00a32.5m reserve set aside for local government reorganisation transitional costs but actual costs remain highly uncertain","lead":"HRA financial plan hinges on grant funding from uncertain bid outcome. LGR transitional costs are contingent and could exceed reserve if staff turnover accelerates or labour market tightens.","iso":"2026-07-07","paper_title":"Budget Setting Context 2027-28","paper_url":"https://democracy.cambridge.gov.uk/documents/s73068/73068.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true},{"headline":"Review of asset compliance across operational estate likely to identify significant unbudgeted immediate capital works requirements","lead":"Unquantified but material capital demands emerging outside the planned capital programme threaten both borrowing capacity and financial sustainability.","iso":"2026-07-07","paper_title":"Budget Setting Context 2027-28","paper_url":"https://democracy.cambridge.gov.uk/documents/s73068/73068.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true},{"headline":"Business rates income shortfall of \u00a31.0m in 2025-26 outturn, with baseline reset removing all previously accumulated growth and significant revaluation appeals pending","lead":"Business rates are a core revenue source; a \u00a31m actual shortfall against budget signals either forecast error or volatile income stream. Future growth potential uncertain following baseline reset.","iso":"2026-07-07","paper_title":"Budget Setting Context 2027-28","paper_url":"https://democracy.cambridge.gov.uk/documents/s73068/73068.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true}],"findings":[{"iso":"2026-07-07","committee":"Cabinet","paper_title":"Budget Setting Context 2027-28","paper_url":"https://democracy.cambridge.gov.uk/documents/s73068/73068.pdf","headline":"Homes England strategic partnership bid outcome pending, with \u00a32.5m reserve set aside for local government reorganisation transitional costs but actual costs remain highly uncertain","detail":"The 30-Year HRA Business Plan assumes success in Homes England bid for strategic partnership to unlock significant capital grant funding. Outcome expected shortly and will require business plan refresh. Separately, LGR transitional costs are estimated at \u00a32.5m reserve but actual costs remain highly uncertain with risks from staff turnover and recruitment impacts.","category":"capital","matters":"HRA financial plan hinges on grant funding from uncertain bid outcome. LGR transitional costs are contingent and could exceed reserve if staff turnover accelerates or labour market tightens.","figures":"\u00a32.5m reserve for LGR transitional costs; grant funding amount unquantified","quote":"the 2026/27 BSR and 30-Year Business Plan assumes that the council will be successful in its bid to Homes England for a strategic partnership which will unlock significant capital grant funding. The outcome of this bid is expected shortly","material":true},{"iso":"2026-07-07","committee":"Cabinet","paper_title":"Budget Setting Context 2027-28","paper_url":"https://democracy.cambridge.gov.uk/documents/s73068/73068.pdf","headline":"Review of asset compliance across operational estate likely to identify significant unbudgeted immediate capital works requirements","detail":"Ongoing review of asset compliance across the council's operational estate is expected to identify immediate requirements for capital works and remediation described as likely to be significant, though the amount is not yet quantified.","category":"capital","matters":"Unquantified but material capital demands emerging outside the planned capital programme threaten both borrowing capacity and financial sustainability.","figures":"Amount described as 'likely to be significant' but unquantified","quote":"The ongoing review of asset compliance across the council's operational estate is likely to identify some immediate requirements for capital works and remediation, which may be significant.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-07-07","committee":"Cabinet","paper_title":"Budget Setting Context 2027-28","paper_url":"https://democracy.cambridge.gov.uk/documents/s73068/73068.pdf","headline":"Business rates income shortfall of \u00a31.0m in 2025-26 outturn, with baseline reset removing all previously accumulated growth and significant revaluation appeals pending","detail":"2025/26 Outturn Report shows business rates income shortfall of almost \u00a31.0m. 2026 revaluation generated significant rating appeals, and baseline reset removed all previously accumulated business rates growth. Uncertainty remains on whether forecast income levels can be achieved, though there is both downside and upside risk.","category":"income","matters":"Business rates are a core revenue source; a \u00a31m actual shortfall against budget signals either forecast error or volatile income stream. Future growth potential uncertain following baseline reset.","figures":"\u00a31.0m shortfall in 2025-26; all business rates growth reset to zero","quote":"there remains significant uncertainty around business rates income following the 2026 revaluation (which has generated significant rating appeals) and baseline reset (which has removed all previously accumulated business rates growth)... the 2025/26 Outturn Report shows a shortfall in business rates income of almost \u00a31.0 million.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-07-07","committee":"Cabinet","paper_title":"Budget Setting Context 2027-28","paper_url":"https://democracy.cambridge.gov.uk/documents/s73068/73068.pdf","headline":"Housing Revenue Account reported \u00a36.9m net operating overspend in 2025-26, mainly repairs and maintenance with Awaab's Law adding significant damp/mould/condensation pressures","detail":"2025/26 HRA overspend of \u00a36.9m driven by repairs and maintenance overspends. Implementation of Awaab's Law has significantly increased costs, particularly around damp, condensation and mould. Void costs remain significantly higher than benchmarks. One-off fire safety waking watch and insurance incidents also contributed.","category":"overspend","matters":"HRA overspends force increased council borrowing to fund the new build housing programme, raising debt costs and crowding out other investment. Demand-led and structural cost drivers are unlikely to reduce soon despite additional \u00a31.7m budget allocation.","figures":"\u00a36.9m 2025-26 net operating overspend; additional \u00a31.7m per year allocated in 2026-27 BSR","quote":"During 2025/26, the HRA reported a net operating overspend of \u00a36.9 million. This was mainly due to overspends on repairs and maintenance. The implementation of Awaab's Law has significantly added to cost pressures in this area, particularly around damp, condensation and mould (DCM).","material":true},{"iso":"2026-07-07","committee":"Cabinet","paper_title":"Budget Setting Context 2027-28","paper_url":"https://democracy.cambridge.gov.uk/documents/s73068/73068.pdf","headline":"Greater Cambridge Development Corporation will take planning powers and is likely to cause significant financial pressure including redundancy costs and loss of planning income","detail":"Government published its response to consultation on establishing a new development corporation for Greater Cambridge in June 2026. The proposal will hand some planning powers to the Development Corporation, likely causing costs for potential redundancies and/or TUPE arrangements, plus loss of a significant portion of the council's planning income.","category":"income","matters":"Loss of planning income is a structural reduction in revenue capacity, combined with unquantified redundancy costs, both reducing available revenue for debt service and service delivery.","figures":"Planning income loss described as 'significant portion', redundancy/TUPE costs undefined","quote":"Whilst the full details are not yet known, this is likely to cause significant financial pressures for the council, including costs associated with potential redundancies and/or TUPE arrangements. It is also likely that the council will lose a significant portion of its planning income in the future.","material":true}],"editorial":null,"freshness":{"last_paper_iso":"2026-07-07"},"name":"Cambridge","findings_total":42,"news_drafts_total":3}}